# lua -- packaged for exactly one consumer, wireplumber, whose entire policy
# layer is Lua scripts.
#
# THE REASON THIS PACKAGE EXISTS AT ALL is a meson subproject. wireplumber's
# `system-lua` option defaults to FALSE, and the false branch is not a fallback
# -- it is an unconditional subproject('lua') call. Its tarball ships
# subprojects/lua.wrap and NO lua source, and that .wrap is download-only
# ([wrap-file] with source_url pointing at lua.org and a patch_url at wrapdb).
# _scripts/build-meson.sh passes --wrap-mode=nodownload, so the default build of
# wireplumber is a hard configure failure here rather than a quiet vendored
# copy. See finding: check the tarball, not the wrap.
#
# The version it would have fetched is 5.4.4, statically linked, which is the
# second reason to package this: a bundled copy would be older than the pin and
# invisible to every check in this tree.
#
# BLFS 12.4, pinned here rather than in versions.env: versions.env is
# regenerated wholesale from the LFS book by tools/pin-versions.sh, so a desktop
# pin placed there is destroyed by the next `make pin`.
#
# md5 81cf5265b8634967d8a7480d238168ce is the book's, checked against the same
# bytes as the sha256 below.
SRC_URL=https://www.lua.org/ftp/lua-5.4.8.tar.gz
SRC_SHA256=4f18ddae154e793e46eeab727c59ef1c0c0c2b744e7b94219710d76f530629ae
SRC_DIR=lua-5.4.8

# No CONFIGURE_ARGS: lua has no configure. It ships a hand-written Makefile with
# one target per platform, and the build and install steps are custom scripts
# for that reason -- see build.sh and install.sh.
#
# The patch in patches/ is BLFS 12.4's lua-5.4.8-shared_library-1.patch, taken
# from the book and vendored rather than fetched, so the build has one fewer
# network dependency and the patch is reviewable in the tree. Upstream lua
# builds ONLY a static liblua.a; nothing could link a shared lua without it, and
# wireplumber's modules are shared objects.
#
# `make linux` and not `make linux-readline`, and the difference matters here
# rather than being a preference. src/Makefile:123 reads
#
#     Linux linux:	linux-noreadline
#
# so the plain target takes no readline dependency at all. readline is packaged
# by another chain; taking it here would add a cross-chain edge for an
# interactive feature that a policy engine's embedded interpreter never uses.
